============================================================================
THE DEUTERIUM ROW: PICKING THE WIDTH BY RULE, NOT BY ACCIDENT
============================================================================

  model prediction   D/H = 2.387 e-5
  LCDM control       D/H = 2.420 e-5
  Cooke observation  D/H = 2.527 +/- 0.030 e-5

[1] The standing row, reproduced
    width  = sqrt(0.030^2 + 0.037^2) = 0.0476
    tension= (2.387 - 2.527)/0.0476 = -2.94 sigma
    LCDM control on the same width          = -2.25 sigma

[2] Option (A): stand on PRIMAT, centre and error both
    This IS the standing row. Its defence is that PRIMAT's central value and
    PRIMAT's 1.10% band on d(d,n)3He are one self-consistent package. Its cost
    is that it ignores a compilation disagreement that is real and measured.
    -> -2.94 sigma

[3] Option (B): treat the compilation spread as a systematic -- BOTH ends
    If the PRIMAT/NACRE disagreement is uncertainty, then the best estimate of
    the rate is the compilation average, and the central D/H must move with it.
    Half the full PRIMAT->NACRE shift, applied to both model and control:

    d(d,n)3He NACRE/PRIMAT = 0.9569, full D/H shift = +0.0524
    half-shift to the average          = +0.0262
    model  D/H 2.387 -> 2.413
    LCDM   D/H 2.420 -> 2.446

    width = sqrt(0.030^2 + 0.0384^2 + 0.0262^2) = 0.0553
    model tension  = -2.06 sigma
    LCDM  tension  = -1.46 sigma

[4] Option (X): the one that must be rejected
    PRIMAT's central value with the spread folded into the error:
      width 0.0713 -> -1.96 sigma
    This is the widest and most flattering construction available, and it is
    the one the rule excludes: it counts the compilation disagreement as
    uncertainty while still standing on one compilation's centre. Taking the
    disagreement seriously means moving the centre too, which is option (B).

[5] What the rule selects

      construction                                    model     LCDM    self-consistent
      (A) PRIMAT centre + PRIMAT error                -2.94    -2.25      yes
      (B) compilation average, centre and error       -2.06    -1.46      yes
      (X) PRIMAT centre + compilation-spread error    -1.96      n/a      NO

    (A) and (B) differ by 0.88 sigma. Both are legitimate; they answer
    different questions. (A) asks how the model does against the best single
    compilation. (B) asks how it does given that the compilations disagree.
    (X) answers neither and is the only one the rule kills outright.

[6] The part that does not move
    The excess deficit -- model minus control -- is width-independent:

      D/H(LCDM) - D/H(model) = 2.420 - 2.387 = 0.033
      in (A) units: 0.69 sigma
      in (B) units: 0.60 sigma
      in (X) units: 0.46 sigma

    The model burns 0.033e-5 more deuterium than the control on identical data,
    and no width choice touches that. Every construction above agrees the model
    sits FURTHER from Cooke than LCDM does. The width debate changes how bad the
    row looks; it does not change the sign or the size of the model's own cost.

============================================================================
VERDICT on #57
============================================================================

    The booking is replaced by a rule: a central value and its error must come from
    the same place. That rule was written before the numbers and it kills exactly one
    construction -- (X), the widest and most flattering, at -1.96 sigma -- because it
    inflates the error with a disagreement whose implication for the centre it then
    ignores.

    Two self-consistent options survive and the corpus should quote BOTH, because they
    answer different questions:
      (A) against the best single compilation:   -2.94 sigma
      (B) allowing that compilations disagree:   -2.06 sigma

    The standing row is (A). It should keep standing, now for a reason rather than by
    the accident of a borrowed error bar, with (B) quoted beside it.

    THE ROW REMAINS ADVERSE EITHER WAY. And the quantity that is width-independent --
    the model burning 0.033e-5 more deuterium than its own control on identical data
    -- is untouched by any of this. Re-booking an error bar does not improve a fit.

